WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:32.060 --> 00:00:36.140
[Dr. Egan:] In women, the breast is the most common site of cancer.

00:00:36.140 --> 00:00:42.870
The incidence is higher than that of all malignant neoplasms of the reproductive organs combined. 

00:00:42.870 --> 00:00:47.610
In the United States, 68,000 new cases are discovered annually. 

00:00:47.610 --> 00:00:48.880
[Line graph of cancers of uterus and breast shown.]

00:00:48.880 --> 00:00:53.030
There are 24,000 deaths from cancer of the breast yearly.

00:00:53.030 --> 00:00:58.610
One of the most striking facts in medicine is that the salvage rate of cancer of the breast has maintained

00:00:58.680 --> 00:01:04.560
 a plateau for the past several decades as shown by the nearly straight line in this graph,

00:01:05.040 --> 00:01:09.520
while in other areas of medicine there have been remarkable advancements,

00:01:09.520 --> 00:01:15.690
as indicated by the dipping line showing a decrease in the death rate from cancer of the uterus. 

00:01:15.690 --> 00:01:20.440
Mammography is a radiographic exploration of the breast. 

00:01:22.520 --> 00:01:29.360
It is a safe, reliable, and aesthetically acceptable method of diagnosis of breast diseases in their early stages.

00:01:29.360 --> 00:01:37.350
Mammography requires no special equipment, and demands only meticulous attention to radiographic technique. 

00:01:37.350 --> 00:01:39.100
Mammography is not new. 

00:01:40.770 --> 00:01:45.450
Salomon in Germany in 1913 used x-rays to study his breast specimens.

00:01:45.450 --> 00:01:54.420
Stafford L. Warren in New York is credited with being the first to apply these procedures clinically in 1929. 

00:01:56.810 --> 00:02:02.800
Leborgne of Uruguay and Gershon-Cohen of the United States have more recently described the examination.

00:02:02.800 --> 00:02:10.310
In the spring of 1956, I was asked to do mammography at the University of Texas

00:02:10.310 --> 00:02:15.830
M.D. Anderson Hospital and Tumor Institute in Houston, Texas. 

00:02:15.830 --> 00:02:21.200
Published techniques gave poor results in my hand.

00:02:21.200 --> 00:02:26.210
As a matter of fact, after talking to a number of the earlier workers in mammography,

00:02:26.210 --> 00:02:37.810
I found that the procedure had fallen into disuse due to the erratic results obtained with poor quality roentgenograms. 

00:02:37.810 --> 00:02:45.950
A wide range of technical factors was investigated prior to adoption of our mammographic technique

00:02:45.950 --> 00:02:51.040
utilizing equipment in the average radiology department having only one variable -

00:02:51.040 --> 00:02:57.760
that of target skin distance and delivering consistently highly diagnostic mammograms. 

00:02:58.590 --> 00:03:01.550
We suggest you master this basic technique.

00:03:01.550 --> 00:03:05.980
Do it for 100 or 1,000 cases.

00:03:05.980 --> 00:03:13.980
Then experiment when you are aware of how much detail is being sacrificed. 

00:03:13.980 --> 00:03:20.420
We shall not be concerned at this time with minor variations of the basic technique.

00:03:20.420 --> 00:03:25.840
All such variations have been tried and found non-rewarding. 

00:03:25.840 --> 00:03:33.060
Presently, we are concerned with emphasizing the need for maximum roentgenographic detail in mammography.

00:03:33.060 --> 00:03:37.680
Diagnosis will be the subject of a future program. 

00:03:37.680 --> 00:03:44.290
Technique is 90 percent of the procedure, with interpretive ability ten percent.

00:03:44.290 --> 00:03:50.260
Historical evaluation as well as our own experience bears this out. 

00:03:50.260 --> 00:03:56.340
A procedure previously tried and discarded in our institution

00:03:56.420 --> 00:04:01.360
immediately became enthusiastically endorsed as a routine procedure

00:04:01.360 --> 00:04:07.560
when the clinicians were presented with a technique of excellent detail. 

00:04:07.560 --> 00:04:11.570
The sine qua non is a film of good detail. 

00:04:11.570 --> 00:04:17.990
And this requires proper coning, fine-grain emulsion, low kilovoltage, high miliampage.

00:04:19.510 --> 00:04:23.630
The technique utilizes only one variable - distance.

00:04:23.680 --> 00:04:29.160
And this compensates for the wide range in size and density in the various breasts. 

00:04:30.720 --> 00:04:34.500
The full examination requires three views: 

00:04:34.600 --> 00:04:39.760
the craniocaudad view, the mediolateral view, and the axillary view.

00:04:39.760 --> 00:04:47.150
Usually the first two views can be included on the five-by-eight half of an eight-by-ten film. 

00:04:47.150 --> 00:04:51.730
With the help of our Chief Mammography Technologist, Ms. Evelyn O'Donnell,

00:04:51.730 --> 00:04:57.340
and our model, we shall run through the procedure of mammography. 

00:04:57.340 --> 00:05:04.820
Detailed facts and factors of the technique will be gladly supplied upon request.

00:05:04.820 --> 00:05:10.660
We also suggest that you spend some time at one of our teaching centers 

00:05:10.660 --> 00:05:15.060
or with an experienced mammographer to polish your technique. 

00:05:15.070 --> 00:05:20.150
A cylinder collimator is necessary to achieve good detail.

00:05:20.150 --> 00:05:26.100
An adjustable diaphragm type of cone is not adequate and must be replaced. 

00:05:26.100 --> 00:05:30.480
Any added tube filtrations must be removed. 

00:05:30.480 --> 00:05:37.260
Thus, the examination is done with the use of only inherent tube filtration. 

00:05:37.260 --> 00:05:45.080
Most cones with adjustable diaphragms have one-half to one millimeter equivalent aluminum filter in the light localizer. 

00:05:45.080 --> 00:05:48.050
Thus, requiring removal. 

00:05:48.050 --> 00:05:53.710
Fine-grain emulsion with adequate contrast range must be used to record detail

00:05:53.710 --> 00:05:59.550
of one-tenth millimeter size objects studied by mammography. 

00:05:59.550 --> 00:06:03.810
Very fine-grain industrial types have been found most suitable. 

00:06:03.810 --> 00:06:07.090
It is used in a cardboard holder. 

00:06:07.090 --> 00:06:10.810
The slight stiffness of the cardboard as opposed to paperbacks

00:06:10.810 --> 00:06:16.570
makes positioning easier with the exclusion of skin wrinkles and air pockets. 

00:06:16.570 --> 00:06:23.870
The kilovoltage setting is one which, at 36 inches with the proper coning,

00:06:23.870 --> 00:06:33.510
just allows penetration of the 15 millimeter aluminum wedge at a setting of 300 milliamperes at six second.

00:06:33.510 --> 00:06:39.770
This kilovoltage is in the neighborhood of 26. 

00:06:39.770 --> 00:06:47.330
Inspection of the roentgenograms of the wedge allows final adjustment of the kilovoltage.

00:06:47.330 --> 00:06:54.970
This will be your setting for the craniocaudad view. Simply increase two KVP for the mediolateral view. 

00:06:54.970 --> 00:07:02.030
If the mammogram is too light, go up two KVP on your average setting.

00:07:02.030 --> 00:07:05.420
If too dark, go down two KVP.

00:07:05.420 --> 00:07:09.530
It is the film effect and reproducibility which is important.

00:07:09.530 --> 00:07:14.820
Not the exact KVP used. 

00:07:14.820 --> 00:07:27.890
Some modifications of the existing x-ray equipment may be necessary to achieve the low KVP and high MAS necessary. 

00:07:27.890 --> 00:07:35.160
For instance, a bypass to the safety control switch or additional taps from an auto transformer

00:07:35.160 --> 00:07:38.620
to get the required low kilovoltage. 

00:07:38.620 --> 00:07:43.960
It is suggested that you check carefully your tube heating and cooling chart

00:07:43.960 --> 00:07:48.600
and figure as though you were getting full MAS delivered. 

00:07:48.610 --> 00:07:54.110
This will be a large safety factor as there is electron fog and fulfillment

00:07:54.110 --> 00:08:04.150
and full MAS is not truly delivered. Set the KVP as low as possible and work up.

00:08:04.150 --> 00:08:12.320
May even start with three-second exposures to make sure this does not totally blacken your film and overload the tube.

00:08:12.320 --> 00:08:16.520
We may now proceed with positioning of the patient.

00:08:16.520 --> 00:08:23.950
It is of extreme importance that the patient is reassured and relaxed. 

00:08:23.950 --> 00:08:27.020
She is seated comfortably at the end of a table,

00:08:27.020 --> 00:08:30.500
adjustable in height, or an adjustable stool

00:08:30.500 --> 00:08:34.780
that may be used in combination with a fixed table. 

00:08:34.780 --> 00:08:38.910
The film is placed flat on the table.

00:08:38.910 --> 00:08:43.710
The cardboard holder, as you just saw, is elevated as high as comfort permits

00:08:43.710 --> 00:08:48.210
with its edge pressed against the chest wall. 

00:08:48.210 --> 00:08:55.950
With the breast placed on the film, half the film is protected by a lead shield

00:08:55.950 --> 00:09:04.420
and will be used for the craniocaudac view of the other breast. An added sandbag adds stability.

00:09:04.420 --> 00:09:14.700
Relaxation and dropping of the shoulder prevents the clavicle from obscuring the base of the breast. 

00:09:14.700 --> 00:09:22.150
The nipple must be exactly in profile; that is. tangential to the x-ray beam. 

00:09:22.150 --> 00:09:27.870
The extreme periphery of the breast will not be included in the craniocaudac view.

00:09:27.870 --> 00:09:35.540
This, however, is of no great concern as these areas will be well-projected on the mediolateral view. 

00:09:35.540 --> 00:09:46.100
The identification marker is placed face up on the axillary portion of the film so it can be read from above.

00:09:46.100 --> 00:09:53.310
If this is always done, the breast can be identified not only as to the superior or lateral aspect,

00:09:53.310 --> 00:09:58.570
but also to the correct breast. 

00:09:58.570 --> 00:10:03.030
The head is turned to the contralateral side.

00:10:03.030 --> 00:10:06.550
The tube is then swung into place. 

00:10:06.550 --> 00:10:10.260
As you note, the cone will be close to the patient's face. 

00:10:10.260 --> 00:10:18.220
The tube is then raised or lowered until the beam covers the breast with a margin of about one inch.

00:10:18.220 --> 00:10:30.890
Some estimate of required target film distance may previously be obtained by exposures of roentgenograms

00:10:30.890 --> 00:10:39.880
or with the use of the filament glow of this non-filtered beam in the subdued light. 

00:10:39.880 --> 00:10:49.630
A central beam is directed to the midpoint of the base of the breast and parallel to the chest wall.

00:10:49.630 --> 00:10:55.810
The more energetic central beam traverses the thickest portion of the breast.

00:10:55.810 --> 00:10:58.190
With fall-off toward the periphery of the breast,

00:10:58.190 --> 00:11:03.460
a homogeneous density on the mammogram is thus produced. 

00:11:03.460 --> 00:11:09.290
If it is desired to study any point of interest on the film more fully,

00:11:09.290 --> 00:11:20.050
the distance can be decreased for more detail study of appropriate regions of the breast. 

00:11:20.050 --> 00:11:28.590
As demonstrated here, the value of the extension cone is apparent.

00:11:28.590 --> 00:11:33.250
The extended cone, by improved collimation,

00:11:33.300 --> 00:11:43.580
compensates for the increased MAS from the decreased target film distance. 

00:11:43.590 --> 00:11:47.460
As a further modification, compression can be used. 

00:11:47.460 --> 00:11:54.350
In practice it is rarely found to be necessary and does require additional gadgetry.

00:11:54.350 --> 00:11:55.850
This is a finished product.

00:11:55.850 --> 00:11:57.300
[An x-ray image of the breast is shown.]

00:11:57.300 --> 00:12:05.290
Note the irregular density in the breast just beneath the areola [?] 

00:12:05.290 --> 00:12:11.800
To review the technical factors for the craniocaudad view: 

00:12:11.800 --> 00:12:15.020
There is no added filtration. 

00:12:15.020 --> 00:12:22.960
We must use proper coning, six-second exposure, industrial fine-grain type film emulsion,

00:12:22.960 --> 00:12:29.000
cardboard holder, 26 kilovoltage, 300 MA,

00:12:29.000 --> 00:12:34.440
and just sufficient distance to cover the breast, usually ranging from 22 to 40 inches. 

00:12:43.330 --> 00:12:47.810
The mediolateral view provides the second projection of the breast. 

00:12:47.810 --> 00:12:52.320
If one view could be considered the most important, this would be it. 

00:12:52.320 --> 00:12:56.520
Also, apparently, it is technically more difficult to master.

00:12:56.520 --> 00:13:03.050
The patient is recumbent with her arm relaxed as shown, her head on the pillow. 

00:13:03.050 --> 00:13:11.290
The film is tucked snugly along the chest wall with the patient in the posterior-oblique position.

00:13:11.290 --> 00:13:17.660
A wooden block is used to support the film. This block is three inches thick. 

00:13:17.660 --> 00:13:21.440
It is important to eliminate skin folds.

00:13:21.440 --> 00:13:26.960
This is best accomplished by elevating the breast and carefully placing the flexible cardboard holder

00:13:26.960 --> 00:13:32.010
to follow snugly the chest wall and lateral surface of the breast. 

00:13:32.010 --> 00:13:36.270
The patient is then adjusted toward the lateral position, if necessary,

00:13:36.270 --> 00:13:40.300
while keeping the breast in good contact with the film holder. 

00:13:40.300 --> 00:13:44.770
This ensures that even a small breast is seen in profile.

00:13:44.770 --> 00:13:48.840
The breast should by supported purely by the wooden block.

00:13:48.840 --> 00:13:51.470
Not suspended from the chest wall. 

00:13:51.470 --> 00:13:54.270
With such support and suspended respiration,

00:13:54.270 --> 00:13:59.570
motion is of no greater problem than in radiography generally. 

00:13:59.570 --> 00:14:02.940
The nipple, as shown, is in profile.

00:14:02.940 --> 00:14:09.710
Half the film is masked and kept in position by a lead shield, and if necessary, an added sandbag.

00:14:09.710 --> 00:14:14.840
A large breast may require an entire eight-by-ten inch film. 

00:14:14.840 --> 00:14:20.100
The central bream is directed to the base of the breast.

00:14:20.100 --> 00:14:24.110
A small portion of the rib cage is included.

00:14:24.110 --> 00:14:27.800
This ensures complete coverage of the breast. 

00:14:27.800 --> 00:14:35.510
The tube is positioned so that the breast is projected on the film plus a border of approximately one inch.

00:14:35.510 --> 00:14:40.120
The patient is relaxed and comfortable. 

00:14:40.120 --> 00:14:44.230
If a pendulous, opposite breast interferes,

00:14:44.230 --> 00:14:46.070
it should be retracted.

00:14:46.070 --> 00:14:49.880
The retracting arm and fingers should be comfortably placed, 

00:14:49.880 --> 00:14:56.530
not with the elbow elevated, nor with the wrist and fingers strained. 

00:14:56.530 --> 00:15:04.140
Pendulous breasts require more care and prevention of skin folds being interposed between the breast and the film holder.

00:15:04.140 --> 00:15:14.110
Now, we shall repeat the positioning of the mediolateral view,

00:15:14.110 --> 00:15:22.390
so that we can emphasize these several points again. 

00:15:22.390 --> 00:15:30.900
As already mentioned, this is the view that generally causes greatest difficulty in positioning.

00:15:30.900 --> 00:15:36.970
This can be seen when the curve set is placed along the chest wall. 

00:15:36.970 --> 00:15:40.680
The patient moves into the lateral position.

00:15:40.680 --> 00:15:45.630
The nipple is nearly in profile. 

00:15:45.630 --> 00:15:54.130
The tube is then brought into position.

00:15:54.130 --> 00:16:02.270
The opposite breast is retracted if necessary. Very gently retracted. 

00:16:02.270 --> 00:16:08.680
The identification marker is placed on the axillary portion of the film, readable from above.

00:16:08.680 --> 00:16:20.460
Cone-down views may be obtained to demonstrate questionable areas to better advantage. 

00:16:20.460 --> 00:16:32.200
Here again, the extension-type cylinder cone is of great value.

00:16:32.200 --> 00:16:38.170
You see extending the cone, if you'll note, there are several markers on the cone.

00:16:38.170 --> 00:16:52.110
These may be conveniently placed for estimation of the area to be covered.

00:16:52.110 --> 00:16:57.260
There is no significant diagnostic difference in the normal mediolateral view 

00:16:57.260 --> 00:17:04.260
as compared with the craniocaudad. 

00:17:04.260 --> 00:17:09.880
However, with only a single view, a cancer such as indicated by the arrows here cannot be localized.

00:17:09.880 --> 00:17:15.000
[An x-ray image of a breast with an up arrow and a down arrow pointing toward a spot on the film is shown.]

00:17:15.000 --> 00:17:17.040
It was not evident clinically. 

00:17:17.040 --> 00:17:23.890
A second view at right angles is required for quadrant localization. 

00:17:23.890 --> 00:17:29.890
The only change in technical factors for the mediolateral view is an added two KVP

00:17:29.890 --> 00:17:33.580
or the setting of 28 KVP.

00:17:33.580 --> 00:17:37.030
No added filtration, proper coning, six seconds, 

00:17:37.030 --> 00:17:43.830
industrial-type film, cardboard holder, 28 KV, 300 MA. 

00:17:44.380 --> 00:17:48.500
Again, the only variable is the change in target film distance

00:17:48.510 --> 00:17:52.830
so that the breast is properly included in the x-ray beam. 

00:17:52.830 --> 00:17:57.370
This usually varies from 22 to 40 inches as on the craniocaudad view. 

00:17:57.370 --> 00:18:04.910
Respiration is suspended during each exposure. 

00:18:08.620 --> 00:18:14.330
The third view taken to complete the mammographic study is the axillary view. 

00:18:14.330 --> 00:18:17.170
The patient is recumbent.

00:18:17.170 --> 00:18:21.930
The entire eight-by-ten film is placed in position. 

00:18:21.930 --> 00:18:27.730
With a contralateral forearm and hand placed comfortably over the abdomen.

00:18:27.730 --> 00:18:33.780
The ipsilateral arm is at a right angle to the trunk with the elbow flexed. 

00:18:33.780 --> 00:18:37.890
Abduction must not be more than 90 degrees.

00:18:37.890 --> 00:18:42.750
Further abduction causes a scapula to be superimposed over the axilla.

00:18:42.750 --> 00:18:45.360
Nor should it be less than 90 degrees.

00:18:45.360 --> 00:18:51.730
Further abduction causes skin folds to be superimposed over the axilla. 

00:18:51.730 --> 00:18:58.540
The patient is then rolled into approximately 30 degrees oblique position. 

00:18:58.540 --> 00:19:03.930
The x-ray beam is centered to a point approximately five centimeters below the apex

00:19:03.930 --> 00:19:13.090
of the axilla with the eight-by-ten inch film centered to the central beam. The breast hangs freely. 

00:19:13.090 --> 00:19:18.520
The target film distance is 40 inches for the average patient. 

00:19:18.520 --> 00:19:24.280
In the obese, increased penetration is achieved

00:19:24.280 --> 00:19:27.800
by decreasing the distance to 30 inches.

00:19:27.800 --> 00:19:31.180
Not by increasing the kilovoltage. 

00:19:31.180 --> 00:19:37.750
With this shorter distance, the centering point is just below the apex of the axilla.

00:19:37.750 --> 00:19:47.270
An identification marker is placed, as on the other view. 

00:19:47.270 --> 00:19:53.530
On the roentgenogram of the axilla can be seen the ribs, humeral head, 

00:19:53.530 --> 00:19:57.490
muscular plains of the axilla, and the retro-mammary space.

00:19:57.490 --> 00:20:02.910
The breast may be distorted in appearance since it is not supported. 

00:20:02.910 --> 00:20:07.240
These technical factors arise from the use of the lowest KVP possible 

00:20:07.240 --> 00:20:09.760
for penetration of the normal axillary tissue. 

00:20:11.140 --> 00:20:17.490
They are: No added filter, proper coning, three and one-half seconds, 

00:20:17.490 --> 00:20:26.660
industrial type film, fine-grain, cardboard holder, 54 KVP, 300 MA, 

00:20:26.660 --> 00:20:30.720
and the average patient's target film distance is 40 inches and in the 

00:20:30.720 --> 00:20:35.610
obese it is reduced to 30 inches. 

00:20:35.610 --> 00:20:41.100
In the obese as noted, the target film distance is reduced to 30 inches,

00:20:41.100 --> 00:20:48.950
covering less area as indicated on the mammogram on the left of your screen.

00:20:48.950 --> 00:21:02.630
This is in comparison with the area covered by the usual 40 inch-distance mammogram on the right of the screen. 

00:21:05.560 --> 00:21:11.000
With any new roentgenographic procedure, dosage rates must be established. 

00:21:11.000 --> 00:21:14.260
From the average exposure during mammography,

00:21:14.260 --> 00:21:24.060
the skin receives with back scanner, 2.4 and 2.8 are as shown in the craniocaudal and the medial lateral views. 

00:21:24.060 --> 00:21:31.800
The total dosage to the midpoint in the average breast for the three exposures is 3.3R. 

00:21:31.980 --> 00:21:35.640
This dose is considered safe. 

00:21:35.640 --> 00:21:38.160
Careful processing of the film is required.

00:21:40.130 --> 00:21:44.470
Developing time of up to eight minutes at 68 degrees Fahrenheit

00:21:44.470 --> 00:21:50.250
with fixing time of ten minutes or more are necessary. 

00:21:50.250 --> 00:21:57.220
A hand magnifying lens is an indispensable aid in viewing the mammograms.

00:21:57.220 --> 00:22:02.180
The detail on the roentgenograms is so fine, the structure is not appreciated with the naked eye;

00:22:02.180 --> 00:22:06.210
and will be appreciated by magnification with this lens. 

00:22:06.210 --> 00:22:13.330
Certain areas of the mammograms may require bright light for optimal viewing. 

00:22:13.330 --> 00:22:18.280
Mammography is not to replace physical examination or biopsy.

00:22:18.280 --> 00:22:23.530
But with good technique, it is a highly accurate procedure. 

00:22:23.530 --> 00:22:29.750
The most important single aspect of a mammography is roentgenographic detail.

00:22:29.750 --> 00:22:35.720
A couple of examples may serve to illustrate the necessity of good mammograms. 

00:22:35.720 --> 00:22:44.410
The first one, shown here with numerous flecks of calcification best seen in the region of this arrow,

00:22:44.410 --> 00:22:51.790
is of a patient who came in complaining of pain in her opposite breast.

00:22:51.790 --> 00:22:56.940
These typical calcifications of cancer were revealed on the - 

00:22:56.940 --> 00:23:02.100
by a mammography in a clinically normal breast. 

00:23:02.100 --> 00:23:14.200
This next patient is that of a rather obese lady who came into the institution with vague breast complaints. 

00:23:14.200 --> 00:23:17.220
The breasts were clinically normal.

00:23:17.220 --> 00:23:27.670
However, with mammography we see in the upper portion of the breast near its base a two-centimeter definite carcinoma. 

00:23:27.670 --> 00:23:35.170
Our third patient is one who had a clinically suspicious nodule in her breast

00:23:35.170 --> 00:23:40.360
seen in the central portion of the breast in this mammogram,

00:23:40.360 --> 00:23:44.910
which was quite clearly carcinoma by mammography.

00:23:44.910 --> 00:23:55.490
However, in the superior and somewhat anteriorly to this mass was a half-centimeter irregular density. 

00:23:55.490 --> 00:23:59.990
A second carcinoma within this breast not appreciated clinically. 

00:23:59.990 --> 00:24:09.950
In the opposite breast there was also a central small lesion approximately six millimeters in size.

00:24:09.950 --> 00:24:19.480
This illustrates the value of a mammography in which we have a suspected carcinoma in one breast,

00:24:19.480 --> 00:24:26.910
we find a second carcinoma in this breast, and a third carcinoma in the same patient in the opposite breast.

00:24:26.910 --> 00:24:31.240
Two clinically unsuspected carcinomas in the one patient.

00:24:31.240 --> 00:24:36.150
This is extreme value in treatment planning. 

00:24:36.150 --> 00:24:38.630
I would like to point out that with mammography

00:24:38.630 --> 00:24:44.880
we have the opportunity to find breast cancers earlier with better prognosis. 

00:24:44.880 --> 00:24:51.670
Hopefully to change that plateau of survival referred to earlier.

00:24:51.670 --> 00:24:57.920
Mammography is nothing but roentgenography based on well-established principles.

00:24:57.920 --> 00:25:02.460
One good mammogram surpasses dozens of poor ones. 

00:25:02.460 --> 00:25:08.390
My suggestion is aim to maintain or improve mammographic detail,

00:25:08.390 --> 00:25:13.140
not attempt shortcuts that may detract from that detail. 

00:25:13.140 --> 00:25:23.560
As mammography will only find its full value if others duplicate and use it.

